of  information  is if we decline the current offer  how long are we expected to wait for a  next offer and what's the relative  quality of that next offer  compared to the current one because we  don't want to wait for nothing we want  to wait for a better offer  and the third point of information is a  final suggestion by the tool to accept  or decline the offer  based on selecting the alternative which  will maximize  survival with a functioning graph so  survival  of dilysis from the time of the initial  office  offer so we  have been working in the past two or  three years  on the first two points uh the graft  survival prediction  and the prediction of wait time if the  offer is refused and  first we will be  talking to you about the expected graft  survival if the offer is accepted  and i will let tristan present this part  which is a very much machine learning  deep learning part all right thank you  louise uh  can you move to the next uh slide please  okay so uh as louise mentioned one of  the big components of the uh  the tool is actually being able to  predict uh the survival  probability of a graft so  uh what we did was uh this is work from  a few years back continuing up to today  what we did was uh we considered the  uh srtr cohort study  and uh we considered single kidney  transplantations  ranging from you know dates between uh  january 1st 2000 and december 31st 2014  and uh we defined a censorship variable  based on  on essentially if the event occurred  after a certain date  and we did not exclude any previous  organ transplants  and the outcome we were predicting  before was  the overall wrap survival which means  that  essentially we're looking at a variable  which is  uh surviv grab does not survive if for  example you have patient death return to  dialysis  or a need for a re-transplantation  and we're considering in this work a set  of variables comprising varicose  pertaining to the recipient  variables pertaining to the donor and  finally variables that are linked to  both  such as the hla mismatch  next slide please so essentially  uh to discuss about this model it's  interesting to go back to the context of  when it was  first uh introduced so essentially what  we did was  uh and this is work dating from 2016  2017  we so at the time we didn't have too  many approaches  on uh deep neural networks applied to  survival analysis in fact we were i  guess  the second work and the first one to  actually introduce  a more complex uh formulation of the fox  loss  the cox log likelihood loss essentially  you have two diff so so this can be seen  as a general machine learning problem  following the  like the following assumptions you have  a target  and you have another variable which is  whether or not your event is censored so  the target would simply be  at this point in time you have an event  an event such as patient death or any  other  any of the other aforementioned events  and censoring simply means that  after this specific point in time  you no longer have any information on  the patient because potentially the  patient has  left the study or has disappeared for  some reason but it does not mean that  the patient has died simply means you  have no information  and what empirically we found is that it  was of course very important to not only  take into account the patients for which  we  have definitive information but the  patients for which we only have partial  information in the form of  all of the survival up to the censoring  time  and what we did was essentially a  multitasking network where we  uh predicted two things in this  formulation  the first one was a ranking glass based  on  a predicted score based on an  intermediate layer of the network  and the second loss was simply a  survival prediction loss based  on uh essentially the the the coxswain  look like the outlaws um  and so of course as input to this model  we have  uh the different features that i  discussed before  uh can you 